# ==============================================================================
# Start the USB4 rescue on a machine that actually has a USB4 controller
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# udev is the part of Linux that notices hardware. Every time a piece of
# hardware turns up, udev reads every rule file like this one and does what the
# matching rules say. This file has one rule, and it says:
#
#     when a PCI device whose class is 0x0c0340 is added,
#     start aquarius-usb4-rescue.service
#
# 0x0c0340 is the number that means "USB4 host interface" — the chip on the
# board that runs the fast USB-C sockets, the ones a Thunderbolt dock or a USB4
# SSD plugs into. Every such chip announces itself with that number, whoever
# made it, so this is a better thing to look for than a list of vendor numbers
# that would go out of date.
#
# What the rescue then does — and the bench fault on 7 September 2026 that it
# exists for — is written out at length in the program's own header,
# /usr/libexec/aquarius-usb4-rescue, and in docs/restart/usb4.md.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ THIS FILE IS THE "SWITCHED ON" MECHANISM. THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO
#    .wants SYMLINK FOR THIS SERVICE.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every other AquariusOS service is switched on by a symlink shipped under /usr
# (the long explanation is beside aq_unit_is_on_from_usr in
# build_files/aq-lib.sh). This one is not, on purpose, and it is worth
# understanding why so nobody "fixes" it later by adding one.
#
# The rescue should run on a machine that HAS a USB4 controller, and should not
# exist at all on a machine that does not. A .wants symlink would start it on
# every machine, every boot, including laptops and handhelds with no such chip,
# where it would find nothing and say so — noise in every journal forever.
#
# udev gives us exactly the "only if the hardware is there" behaviour for free.
# The one thing a newcomer worries about here is: "surely this only fires when
# somebody plugs the chip in, and nobody plugs in a chip that is soldered to the
# board?" — and the answer is no. At every boot, systemd-udev walks all the
# hardware that is ALREADY present and replays an "add" event for each piece of
# it. That is called coldplug, it is how most of the hardware on a Linux machine
# gets set up, and it means this rule fires on every boot of a machine with the
# chip and on no boot of a machine without it.
#
# And because this file lives in /usr/lib/udev/rules.d — the read-only half of
# the system, replaced whole at every update — no local change can lose it, and
# no `systemctl disable` can turn it off. `sudo systemctl mask
# aquarius-usb4-rescue.service` is the off switch, exactly as for our other
# units.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT EACH PIECE OF THE LINE MEANS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ACTION=="add"            only when hardware appears (which includes the
#                            coldplug replay at every boot), never on removal
#   SUBSYSTEM=="pci"         a chip on the board, not a USB stick
#   ATTR{class}=="0x0c0340"  it is a USB4 host interface
#   TAG+="systemd"           tell systemd this device exists, which is what
#                            makes the next line possible at all
#   ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+=     "when this device turns up, please start this
#                            service" — systemd's own supported way for a piece
#                            of hardware to pull in a unit
# ==============================================================================

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="pci", ATTR{class}=="0x0c0340", TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="aquarius-usb4-rescue.service"
